README-KEEP -- taking things off this collection================================================    keep <name>...      the program, and whatever it needs    keep -n <name>...   dry run: say what would be written, touch nothing    keep -f <name>...   overwrite files already there (otherwise refused)    keep -s <name>...   take the SOURCE tree as well, where there is one    keep -p <dir> ...   put it all under <dir> instead of /h1    kept                every file keep has written, and the total    unkeep <name>...    remove what keep wrote for <name>    unkeep -n <name>... dry run    unkeep -a <name>... remove its files, and your saves and scores tooOne arrangement, for running and for keeping--------------------------------------------This collection is mounted as /dd, and as /h0 as well; your own OS-9 systemis mounted as /h1.  That is what DOC/README-RUNNING describes, and it is thesame arrangement whether you are running programs or keeping them.Everything runs in place from /dd, and for that you need none of this.`keep' is for taking a program home.  It copies the program and the filesDOC/DEPENDS says it reads from this collection onto your own disk on /h1,laid out the way the program expects to find them: a program that reads/dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz gets /h1/GAMES/adv/glorkz, so that when you boot yourown disk as /dd again it finds its file where it looks.Where it copies from and to---------------------------From the collection, which it finds by looking for DOC/DEPENDS on /dd, /h0,/h1 and /h2 in that order; to /h1.  To say otherwise:    -p <dir>            on the command line    KEEP_FROM, KEEP_TO  in the environmentSet those INSIDE an OS-9 session -- `export KEEP_TO=/h2' at a shell here.A variable set in the shell that launched os9exec does not reach an OS-9program; that is a property of the emulator, not of these commands.Taking the source too---------------------`keep -s' brings the program's source tree along with it.  Where the sourcesurvived, you get it; where it did not, keep says so and takes the rest.Much of this collection has no source at all -- that is not a fault, it iswhat reached us.The tree is named by ARCHIVE, not by program.  SRC/divutils holds `gen',`run' and `if'; SRC/adv holds `advent'.  Guessing SRC/<program> findsnothing for most of the disk, so keep looks the name up in DOC/ORIGINS.`about <program>' shows you the same answer before you commit to anything.What `keep' takes with it--------------------------Not just the binary.  DOC/DEPENDS lists, for every program, each absolutepath its code contains -- generated by scanning the binaries, not byguesswork -- and keep brings any of those that exist here.    keep cookie     takes cookie, its sayings and its sayhash    keep fortune    takes fortune and its fortunes.dat    keep hack       takes hack and its whole playground -- data, help,                    rumors, the bones files, and room to save intoA dependency that is a DATA DIRECTORY is copied whole: hack's playground,larn's PLAYGROUND of maze, fortune and score files, sokoban's screens, comewith the program so the game has its data where it looks for it.A dependency that is a TOP-LEVEL system directory -- /dd/cmds, /dd/sys,/dd/usr, /dd/lib, /dd/log -- is NOT copied.  Your own disk has its own, andthe collection's /dd/cmds is every program here; a tool that merely namesone of these (adduser names /dd/cmds, gcc names /dd/lib) does not need thecollection's copy of it, and overwriting yours with ours is the clobberingkeep exists to avoid.  keep names it and leaves it to your disk.A file named twice is taken once.  A program that wants both /dd/sys/termcapand /h0/sys/termcap is asking for one file.Termcap is copied WHEN YOUR DISK HAS NONE.  The curses programs need aterminal description, and on this collection SYS/login points TERMCAP atSYS/termcap; your own system may have neither, so keep brings one -- andleaves yours exactly as it is if you already have it.Nothing of yours is overwritten.  A file keep itself wrote before, unchanged,is already right and is left (`already kept'); one you have changed since --a score, a saved game -- is yours now and is left too.  A file of your ownunder the same name is never replaced silently; keep names it and moves on.`-f' overrides that.  So keeping a program a second time is safe: it restoresanything missing and touches nothing you have made your own.Data files are written PUBLICLY WRITABLE, and that matters.  Everything hereis owned 0.0, so anyone logged in as somebody else is never the owner andonly the public write bit counts.  A read-only score file is a game thatcannot save, and testing as 0.0 hides it, because RBF gives the super-user abypass.Why `unkeep' is safe--------------------Every file keep writes is recorded in SYS/kept on the destination disk(/h1/SYS/kept unless -p or KEEP_TO said otherwise): the program it came for,where it went, its size, and its checksum.unkeep reads that receipt and, for each file:    gone already            says so, moves on    another program needs it  LEAVES IT, drops only this program's claim    checksum matches        removes it    CHECKSUM CHANGED        KEEPS IT, and tells youThe third rule is what makes a shared file safe.  termcap is the one thatmatters: keep two curses programs and both record it; unkeep one and theother still has its terminal description, because its claim is still on thereceipt.  Only when the last program that needed it is unkept does the fileitself go.The fourth rule is what makes your own work safe.  A file you have written tois, by definition, no longer the file keep put there -- so unkeep will nottouch it.  Your saves, your scores, your edited configuration survive`unkeep' by construction, not by our being careful.  When you do want themgone as well -- a clean sweep, not a rescue -- `unkeep -a' removes thechanged files too.  Demonstrated:    $ keep fortune    collection at /h0, keeping into /dd    fortune:      /dd/CMDS/fortune      /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat    2 files written.    $ echo "A fortune I added myself." >> /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat    $ unkeep fortune    fortune:      removed /dd/CMDS/fortune      !! /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat has CHANGED since it was kept -- left alone         (this is how your saves and high scores survive an unkeep)    1 removed, 1 left alone.A file unkeep declines to remove stays on the receipt, so `kept' still knowsabout it and a later `unkeep' -- or `unkeep -a' -- will offer it again.The checksum is the OS-9 module CRC -- polynomial $800063, complemented --used here on ordinary files too.  Any checksum would do; that one is alreadythe collection's idiom and its other tools understand it.How they are written--------------------Three OS-9 modules, built from one source, SRC/keep/keep.c, with `cc -qm' sothey carry no trap handler and need no cio.  They fork directly, like anyother command here.They began as bash scripts, and those are kept in SRC/keep as keep.sh,unkeep.sh and kept.sh.  They were replaced because a script could not do theone job that matters: it needs bash and /dd/tmp ON THE DESTINATION disk, andthe destination is somebody else's OS-9 system that has neither.  Run againsta /dd that was not the collection, the script version copied nothing and saidalmost nothing about why.  This bash is also a difficult thing to writeagainst -- command substitution does not inherit PATH or see shell functions,the -f and -d tests are unreliable on a host-directory mount andcase-sensitive where OS-9 is not, there is no /dev/null, and mkdir has no -p.The C version has none of those problems.